\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Seven threats to the global economy: from inflation to cryptocurrencies\b0\par\par\uc2 1,206 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Dezember 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 A crisis initially confined to a Lego theme park ended up hitting the entire South Korean economy. It happened in early autumn. The developer of the leisure center, which is owned by the Gangwon provincial government, defaulted on a 137 million euro bond. The decision not only put the project on the ropes. In a market with less liquidity due to rising interest rates, the arteries through which corporate credit flows were frozen and the Government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Korea were forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Legoland default has remained, at least for now, a scare. Just another one. Almost parallel to this crisis, the City of London was plunged into chaos after former Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  revealed her plans to cut taxes. The turbulence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 United Kingdom\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which also required the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 , once again highlighted the fragility of the global economy.\par \par \uc2 In the post-cool era, there are several latent threats. And the combination of two or three shocks can jeopardize financial stability. In the UK, market mistrust, lower liquidity and the scale of shadow banking came together. "Welcome to the world of the polycrisis," wrote historian \uc2 Adam Tooze\uc2  of \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  in the \uc2 Financial Times\uc2  recently, borrowing a term used by former \uc2 European Commission\uc2  President \uc2 Jean-Claude Juncker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The most repeated word in all the forecasts formulated by international organizations is uncertainty. It appears five times in the \uc2 European Commission\uc2 's forecasts, 30 times in those of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ) and up to 96 times in those of the \uc2 OECD\uc2 . After the post-pandemic rebound, projections point to the economy cooling down again. The latest, from the \uc2 OECD\uc2 , indicate that the world will grow by 2.2% in 2023, compared to 3.1% this year. The slowdown will be felt above all in the United States and Europe, whose economies are expected to grow by barely 0.5%. This time, the crisis is based on several phenomena. Some are visible and others are latent; some are fading and others are gaining in intensity. And the danger is that some will continue to interact with the others.\par \par \uc2 Inflation. It stands as the main enemy of international institutions. "The number one priority remains the fight against inflation. We will not be able to sustain economic growth without restoring price stability," says Daniel Leigh, director of the IMF's World Economic Research Division. Although price increases in both the US (7.7%) and Europe (10%) moderated in November, the level reached is still very high. The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (\uc2 ECB\uc2 ) chief economist, \uc2 Philip Lane\uc2 , said this week that the "peak" could be near, but also warned that prices will remain high in 2023 and 2024. Markets expect inflationary escalation to moderate and central banks to ease interest rate hikes, which could even unleash another threat to the economy: recession.\par \par \uc2 Recession. The autumn has turned out to be less gray than expected on both sides of the Atlantic, but forecasts still point to a slowdown in the economy by 2022. Even a technical recession for the eurozone. This week, the \uc2 OECD\uc2  warned that leading indicators for most advanced economies reflected a loss of momentum in activity. "We expect to see several different shades of recession in 2023\u8243\'20\'33, says \uc2 Carsten Brzeski\uc2 , global head of Macro at ING. That, he adds, translates into a "textbook" depression in the US triggered by the Fed to cool the economy and a milder one in the eurozone. The objective seems to be clear: to avoid stagflation at all costs.\par \par \uc2 Climate and energy crisis. \uc2 Carsten Brzeski\uc2  believes that Europe may be heading into a recession because of the "structural challenges and transitions" it faces. The \uc2 EU\uc2  intends to step on the accelerator to move away from fossil fuels in view of the high cost of dependence on Russian gas. According to the \uc2 OECD\uc2 , energy expenditure is equivalent to 17.7% of GDP, a level unseen since the two oil crises. It is this bill that has boosted the inflation rate to levels never before seen in the euro. International organizations warn of another danger arising from climate change: the proliferation of new pandemics, such as the cholera outbreaks already detected in 30 countries.\par \par \uc2 Debt. The \uc2 IMF\uc2  estimated in 2021 that world debt reached 226 trillion euros, or 256% of global GDP. Public debt, which was equivalent to all the world's wealth, accelerated. In these parameters is also Europe, where the obligations of the countries ceased to be a problem while rates remained below zero. "The \uc2 ECB\uc2  has bought large amounts of government bonds while rates have been in negative territory," says \uc2 London School of Economics\uc2  professor and former Italian Treasury Secretary \uc2 Lorenzo Codogno\uc2 . That era is long gone, and rate hikes are putting that mountain of debt back on the risk map.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine. No roadmap can dismiss geopolitical risks any longer. This year there were two on the analysts' agenda: the war in Ukraine and the U.S. elections, where the Democrats have managed to save the furniture. "War is the main factor today in Europe. And almost everything is connected to that conflict," says \uc2 Nicolas Veron\uc2 , a researcher at Bruegel and the \uc2 Peterson Institute for International Economics\uc2 . Russia's attacks triggered a crisis in commodity and energy markets that amplified inflationary pressures and reversed the economic recovery process, especially in Europe.\par \par \uc2 China. The recent protests in the streets of the Asian giant's main cities were seen just a few weeks ago as a new source of instability. However, it took just hours for the markets to turn from red to green. In just one day, they saw that these demonstrations were going to be the catalyst for the country's authorities to start lifting the strict restrictions imposed by the zero covid policy. "China is going to grow by 5.5%, more than we expected. Europe is going to benefit less from this development, because it will mean more demand for energy and will keep prices higher than we thought," says \uc2 Alicia Garc\u237\'00\'EDa-Herrero\uc2 , chief economist at \uc2 Natixis\uc2 . Doubts persist, however, about the country's real estate bubble, which has already shown several signs of fatigue.\par \par \uc2 Shadow banking and cryptocurrencies. Institutions have identified all of the above risks. The concern lies, however, in what they do not see: all the assets that remain in the shadows, off the banks' balance sheets. Last October, the \uc2 European Systemic Risk Board\uc2  issued for the first time since its creation in 2010 a warning by identifying "a number of serious risks to financial stability". The economic downturn, in its view, could lead to the emergence of bubbles such as real estate, but also of products deposited with non-financial institutions. "There is a growing financial risk, which could provoke another major shock," says Garc\u237\'00\'EDa-Herrero. Central banks are on their guard. The Bank for International Settlements (BIS) has warned that there is 65 trillion euros of debt hidden in off-balance sheet \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions. \uc2 ECB\uc2  President \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  just this week recalled the collapse of cryptocurrency platforms such as FTX. And there are still many carpets to be lifted.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020221211eicb002wp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw442\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6630\pichgoal600
