\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Pound crash forces \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  and Truss government to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 599 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The confrontation unleashed between the fiscal savings package announced by the \uc2 British government\uc2  to tackle the recession and the rate hikes pushed by the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  to tackle galloping inflation threaten to end the parity between the pound and the dollar and force a large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the price of money in the \b \highlight22\uc2 United Kingdom\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The British \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  suffered a mini-crash yesterday that dragged it down to an all-time low of $1.0350. The crash, which lasted only a few minutes - a flash crash, in the jargon - set off alarm bells and forced the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  and the \uc2 British government\uc2  to step in.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2  assured that it "will not hesitate to change interest rates if it deems it necessary", although it opted not to make an emergency increase in the price of money. At the close of Europe, the fall of the pound eased to 1.95%, standing at 1.0647 dollars. The market is still not ruling out an extraordinary meeting of the institution, prior to the November meeting, to raise interest rates, a measure ruled out yesterday by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which last week raised the price of money by 50 basis points, below market expectations. "We will make a full assessment, at the next scheduled meeting, of the impact on demand and inflation of the announcements made by the government, as well as the fall in sterling, and act accordingly," the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  said. At the same time, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  promised to publish a plan to reduce indebtedness and reaffirmed its budgetary rigor.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 British government\uc2  of \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  presented on Friday the biggest tax cut package since 1972. Measures that Finance Minister \uc2 Kwasi Kwarteng\uc2  assured over the weekend would be extended and that the market has interpreted as more wood for inflation -9.9% in August-, higher public debt and further rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 UBS\uc2  experts believe that in view of the lack of clarity given by the British Executive "on how this stimulus will be financed, investors have added a risk premium to UK assets, including the pound". They further note that to compound the pound's problems the UK's current account balance has entered negative territory and the current account deficit could exceed 5% of GDP by 2022.\par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  estimated that the pound's evolution depends on the monetary response against inflation in the short term and "well-targeted fiscal measures. So far, however, results have been weak on both fronts". Hence, it has lowered its three-month estimate for the pound to 1.05 against the dollar from 1.14 previously.\par \par \uc2 Azad Zangana, economist and senior Europe strategist at \uc2 Schroders\uc2 , believes that the magnitude of the fiscal stimulus announced by London will force the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  to raise rates more than expected. In fact, the market consensus already expects it to raise it by 100 basis points in November. At Ebury's, they estimate that the price of money will be close to 6% in 2023 in the UK.\par \par \uc2 "This plunge demonstrates the lack of market confidence in the UK and a significant weakening of its financial strength," notes Franco Macchiavelli, analyst at Activotrade. A message that \uc2 Citi\uc2  strategists sum up in one sentence: the dollar king reigns, and which they expect to be maintained until the release of new US employment figures.\par \par \uc2 Beyond its cross with the pound sterling, the dollar's strength is starting to be a problem for the world's major economies. The\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220926ei9q009cy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw109\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1635\pichgoal600
