l                                        \u163\'00\'A3     24,427 \u163\'00\'A3 15,663 \u163\'00\'A3 12,022\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table sets forth our consolidated statements of comprehensive income data expressed as a percentage of total revenue:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fiscal Year Ended June 30,\par 2021                                                                             2020    2019\par Consolidated Statements of Comprehensive Income Data:\par Revenue                                                                          100.0%  100.0%  100.0%\par Cost of sales:\par Direct cost of sales                                                             (60.9)% (66.5)% (60.5)%\par Allocated cost of sales                                                          (4.7)%  (5.0)%  (5.2)%\par Total Cost of sales                                                              (65.5)% (71.5)% (65.7)%\par Gross profit                                                                     34.5%   28.5%   34.3%\par Selling, general and administrative expenses                                     (20.2)% (21.4)% (22.9)%\par Net impairment losses on financial assets                                        -%      (0.9)%  -%\par Operating profit                                                                 14.2%   6.2%    11.5%\par Net finance income/(expense)                                                     (2.1)%  0.3%    (1.0)%\par Gain on sale of subsidiary                                                       -%      0.6%    -%\par Profit before tax                                                                12.2%   7.2%    10.5%\par Tax on profit on ordinary activities                                             (2.4)%  (1.1)%  (2.1)%\par Profit for the year and profit attributable to the equity holders of the Company 9.7%    6.1%    8.3%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Adoption of IFRS 16 Leases\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fiscal year 2020 was the first fiscal year in which we have prepared our financial statements following adoption of IFRS 16 Leases. The application of IFRS 16 has resulted in a material gross up of the Consolidated Balance Sheet and a reclassification of charges previously booked to cost of sales and operating expenses to depreciation and interest expense. The impact on the Consolidated Statement of Comprehensive Income is not significant as included in the allocated cost of sales is the portion of depreciation and amortization expense attributable to the portion of our property and equipment and intangible assets utilized in the delivery of services to our clients, including depreciation of right-of-use assets. The net impact on profit before tax is immaterial and the approach to adopting the new standard has not required comparative information to be restated.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Comparison of the Years Ended June 30, 2021 and 2020\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Revenue\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Year Ended June 30, % Change\par 2021                2020 2021 vs.2020\par (pounds in thousands)\par Revenue             \u163\'00\'A3    446,298 \u163\'00\'A3 350,950 27.2%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2021 Compared to 2020.Revenue for 2021 was \u163\'00\'A3446.3 million, an increase of \u163\'00\'A395.3 million, or 27.2%, over 2020. In constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  terms, revenue grew by 29.6% over 2020. We achieved significant growth in revenue across all verticals. Revenue from clients in the Payments and Financial Services vertical increased by \u163\'00\'A341.2 million, or 22.3%, to \u163\'00\'A3226.4 million in 2021 from \u163\'00\'A3185.2 million in 2020. Revenue from clients in the TMT vertical increased by \u163\'00\'A330.8 million, or 34.1%, to \u163\'00\'A3121.0 million in 2021 from \u163\'00\'A390.3 million in 2020. Revenue from clients in our Other vertical also grew significantly, increasing by \u163\'00\'A323.3 million, or 30.9%, to \u163\'00\'A398.9 million in 2021 from \u163\'00\'A375.5 million in 2020. The acquired \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of CDS contributed \u163\'00\'A327.2 million in 2021, particularly within our\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other vertical and in Europe. The acquired \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Five contributed \u163\'00\'A34.8 million in 2021, particularly within our Other and TMT verticals and in North America. The acquired \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Levvel contributed \u163\'00\'A311.6 million in 2021, particularly within our Other and Payments and Financial Services verticals and in North America. Revenue also grew across all geographies. Revenue from clients based in Europe increased by \u163\'00\'A322.1 million, or 25.7%, to \u163\'00\'A3108.0 million in 2021 from \u163\'00\'A385.9 million in 2020. Revenue from clients based in the United Kingdom increased by \u163\'00\'A331.5 million, or 20.3%, to \u163\'00\'A3187.0 million in 2021 from \u163\'00\'A3155.5 million in 2020. Revenue from clients based in North America increased by \u163\'00\'A340.0 million, or 40.0%, to \u163\'00\'A3140.1 million in 2021 from \u163\'00\'A3100.1 million in 2020. Revenue from clients based in Rest of World increased by \u163\'00\'A31.7 million, or 18.1%, to \u163\'00\'A311.2 million in 2021 from \u163\'00\'A39.5 million in 2020. Revenue from our top 10 clients in 2021 increased by \u163\'00\'A322.1 million, or 16.6%, to \u163\'00\'A3155.9 million compared to \u163\'00\'A3133.8 million in revenue from our top 10 clients in 2020.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cost of Sales\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Year Ended June 30,     % Change\par 2021                    2020     2021 vs.2020\par (pounds in thousands)\par Cost of sales\par Direct cost of sales    \u163\'00\'A3        (271,707) \u163\'00\'A3 (233,352) 16.4%\par Allocated cost of sales (20,758) (17,447)  19.0%\par Total Cost of sales     \u163\'00\'A3        (292,465) \u163\'00\'A3 (250,799) 16.6%\par Gross margin            34.5%    28.5%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2021 Compared to 2020.Total cost of sales increased by \u163\'00\'A341.7 million, or 16.6%, in 2021 compared to 2020. The increase consisted of a \u163\'00\'A338.4 million increase in direct cost of sales, as a result of increased personnel costs, which reflected an increase in the average number of employees involved in delivery of our services from 5,633 in 2020 to 6,943 in 2021. Our growth in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  headcount consisted of new employees located in Europe in connection with our acquisitions of CDS and Five and new employees in the Americas in connection with our acquisitions of Five and Levvel. Grant income decreased by \u163\'00\'A30.2 million in 2021 compared to 2020 and research and development credits (in respect of innovative work we carried out for contract customers) increased by \u163\'00\'A31.0 million in 2021 compared to 2020. Included in the allocated cost of sales is the portion of depreciation and amortization expense attributable to the portion of our property and equipment and intangible assets utilized in the delivery of services to our clients. This increased by \u163\'00\'A33.3 million in 2021 compared to 2020, or 19.0% due to the increase in size of our delivery organization. Gross margin increased to 34.5% in 2021 from 28.5% in 2020.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Selling, General and Administrative Expenses\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Year Ended June 30,                          % Change\par 2021                                         2020   2021 vs.2020\par (pounds in thousands)\par Selling, general and administrative expenses \u163\'00\'A3      (90,290) \u163\'00\'A3      (75,110) 20.2%\par % of revenue                                 (20.2) %        (21.4) %\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2021 Compared to 2020.Selling, general and administrative expenses increased by \u163\'00\'A315.2 million, or 20.2%, in 2021 compared to 2020. The increase in total selling, general and administrative expenses is primarily related to an increase of \u163\'00\'A35.0 million in general and administrative expenses as a result of increased support functions costs in line with growth, increased merger and acquisition costs and Sarbanes-Oxley compliance expenses. Sales and marketing expenses increased by \u163\'00\'A34.0 million. Depreciation and amortization increased by \u163\'00\'A33.1 million, or 50.0%, in 2021 compared to 2020, primarily as a result of a \u163\'00\'A32.7 million increase in amortization of acquired intangible\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 assets acquired. As a percentage of revenue, selling, general and administrative expenses decreased from 21.4% to 20.2%. Excluding the non-recurring cost of the discretionary EBT bonus, selling, general and administrative expenses as a percentage of revenue in 2020 would have been 21.6%. Fiscal year 2021 on fiscal year 2020 selling, general and administrative expenses as a percentage of revenue has reduced.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Net impairment losses on financial assets\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Year Ended June 30,                       % Change\par 2021                                      2020 2021 vs.2020\par (pounds in thousands)\par Net impairment losses on financial assets \u163\'00\'A3    (4) \u163\'00\'A3     (3,169) (99.9)%\par % of revenue                              -    %   (0.9) %\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2021 Compared to 2020.Net impairment losses on financial assets declined by \u163\'00\'A33.2 million, or 99.9%. in 2021 compared to 2020. In fiscal year 2020, expected credit losses were charged against clients based on the aging of their debtor balance and an analysis of the debtors' current financial position. In addition, due to the impact of the COVID-19 pandemic, expected credit losses were charged against clients linked to industry sectors that we consider most heavily affected by the pandemic. In fiscal year 2021 the aggregate charge was lower compared to fiscal year 2020 due to changes in debtor balances and client financial positions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Net Finance Income/(Expense)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Year Ended June 30,          % Change\par 2021                         2020  2021 vs.2020\par (pounds in thousands)\par Net finance income/(expense) \u163\'00\'A3     (9,184) \u163\'00\'A3   1,169 (885.6) %\par % of revenue                 (2.1) %       0.3 %\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2021 Compared to 2020.In 2021, we recognized net finance expense of \u163\'00\'A39.2 million, which included a charge to lease interest of \u163\'00\'A31.2 million and a \u163\'00\'A36.5 million expense related to changes in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gain from Sale of Subsidiary\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Year Ended June 30,          % Change\par 2021                         2020 2021 vs.2020\par (pounds in thousands)\par Gain from sale of subsidiary \u163\'00\'A3    - \u163\'00\'A3   2,215 100.0 %\par % of revenue                 -    % 0.6 %\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2021 compared to 2020.On June 1, 2019, Endava entered into an agreement to sell the Captive to Worldpay and to terminate the option and transfer agreement that had been in effect between Endava and Worldpay. On August 31, 2019 the transaction was completed and the employees of the Captive became employees of Worldpay. The aggregate selling price of the Captive was \u163\'00\'A33.6 million and we recognized a gain on disposal of subsidiary of \u163\'00\'A32.2 million. No subsidiaries were sold in 2021.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Provision for Income Tax\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Year Ended June 30,        % Change\par 2021                       2020 2021 vs.2020\par (pounds in thousands)\par Provision for income taxes \u163\'00\'A3    (10,914) \u163\'00\'A3 (3,846) 183.8 %\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2021 Compared to 2020.Provision for income taxes increased by \u163\'00\'A37.1 million, or 183.8%, in 2021 compared to 2020. Our annual effective tax rate for 2021 was 20.1%, compared to an annual effective tax rate of 15.2% for 2020. The 2020 effective tax rate benefited from the non-taxability of the gain on the sale of the Captive and one-off tax measures introduced by governments in response to the COVID-19 pandemic that were not repeated in 2021.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Comparison of the Years Ended June 30, 2020 and 2019\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A comparison of fiscal years 2020 and 2019 can be found in Item 5.A-Operating Results" in our Annual Report on Form 20-F for the fiscal year ended June 30, 2020, which was filed with the SEC on September 15, 2020.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 B.Liquidity and Capital Resources.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Capital Resources\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To date, we have financed our \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  primarily through sales of information technology services, as well as borrowings under our revolving credit facilities and through our initial public offering, which we completed in July 2018. As of June 30, 2021, we had \u163\'00\'A369.9 million in cash and cash equivalents.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In October 2019, we entered into the Facility Agreement. The Facility Agreement is an unsecured revolving credit facility in the amount of \u163\'00\'A3200 million with an initial period of three years, and it replaced a previous \u163\'00\'A350 million secured facility with HSBC UK Bank Plc. The Facility Agreement also provides for uncommitted accordion options for up to an aggregate of \u163\'00\'A375 million in additional borrowing. The Facility Agreement is intended to support the Company's and its subsidiaries' future capital investments and development activities. The Facility Agreement was extended for a further year in October 2020 and currently matures on October 12, 2023. Loans under the Facility Agreement bear interest, at our option, at a rate equal to either the LIBOR rate, the EURIBOR rate or the ROBOR rate, plus an applicable margin ranging from 0.8% to 1.50% per annum, based upon the net leverage ratio. Our obligations under the Facility Agreement are guaranteed by some of our subsidiaries. The Facility Agreement contains customary representations and warranties and customary affirmative and negative covenants applicable to the facility parties and our consolidated subsidiaries. Under the terms of the Facility Agreement, we are required to comply with net leverage ratio and interest coverage covenants. The Facility Agreement contains customary events of default. As of June 30, 2021, there was no amount outstanding under the \u163\'00\'A3200 million primary facility apart from \u163\'00\'A318.4 million utilized for bank guarantees issued by HSBC UK Bank plc, and we were not in breach of any covenants.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On completion of our initial public offering, we received \u163\'00\'A340.2 million net proceeds. A portion of the net proceeds were used to repay all amounts borrowed under our previous revolving credit facility in August 2018.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Future Capital Requirements\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 We believe that our existing cash and cash equivalents, together with cash generated from our \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , will be sufficient to meet our working capital expenditure requirements for at least the next 12 months. Our future capital requirements will depend on many factors, including our growth rate and any acquisitions we may complete.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Contractual Obligations and Commitments\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table summarizes our commitments to settle contractual obligations as of June 30, 2021 and the effect such obligations are expected to have our liquidity and cash flows:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Less than 1 Year                         1 to 3Years 3 to 5Years More than 5 Yea