-------- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chairman's statement\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Overview\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The year to 31 March 2013 has seen growth in two important measures of the business for the first time in five years: growth in assets under management equivalents and growth in client numbers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although we have maintained broad stability in our fee rates in our main product lines, this year has seen the full effect of the change in the product mix towards \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hedging and away from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for return. Within \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hedging, we have also seen stronger growth in passive products. This has meant that our blended fee rate has fallen. This evolution of the product mix has led to the higher-fee \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for return products now representing just 5% of total AUME compared to 43% of total AUME in the year ended 31 March 2009. In contrast, hedging AUME has increased from 54% to 95% of total AUME over the same period. As a result of this change in product mix, the average composite management fee rate has decreased from 17.1bps to 8.8bps, giving an indication of the challenges the business has faced over this extended period, and the pressure exerted on overall profit and revenues as a result.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During the year the external environment began to show some more benign features, particularly in the second half. In many ways, this reflected the more generally benign equity and credit markets over the period. The marked weakness of the Japanese Yen in the second half of the financial year, in particular, was positive for both our hedging and our \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for return products. However, the firm is cognisant that financial recovery may be temporary or fragile given the vulnerable state of many developed economies' public finances, and the extreme tensions within the Eurozone. [ 11-06-13 0600GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The resulting good performance for our \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for return products; in particular our emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  product (which has now reached its third anniversary) has piqued interest amongst institutional and wealth management investors. Record managed to secure its first multi-strategy \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  mandate during the year from an institutional investor - adding a further product offering to the investor marketplace.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financial highlights\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Against this backdrop Record's total revenues have fallen by 9% to GBP18.6 million, achieving an overall profit before tax of GBP6.1 million, down 9% on the year to 31 March 2012. The business has mitigated this fall in revenue in part through the success of a cost reduction programme initiated in November 2011 and the operating profit margin has remained at 32% for the year. The net client AUME inflows for the year were positive at +$1.9 billion (2012: +$0.2 billion) and AUME ended the year at $34.8 billion (2012: $30.9 billion), an increase of 13%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dividend\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As indicated in the interim report, the Board is recommending a final dividend of 1.50p per share, payable on 31 July 2013 to shareholders on the register at 21 June 2013. The total dividend in respect of the year ended 31 March 2013 of 1.50p per share is unchanged on the previous year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the current financial year, the Board's intention, subject to business conditions, is to retain the overall dividend payable at 1.50p per share, which the Board would expect to be payable equally in respect of an interim and final dividend, in line with prior financial periods. In setting the dividend, the Board will be mindful of achieving a level which it expects to be covered by earnings.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Group strategy\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During the risk-averse response to the financial crisis, we identified our hedging products as offering the most likely source of growth in AUME in the short term. Consequently marketing effort and resource were mainly focussed on maintaining and growing the assets in these products. This strategy, while facing a continuing difficult sales environment, succeeded in growing our AUME in both Dynamic and Passive Hedging during the year. As already mentioned, we now also have a three-year track record for our Emerging Market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  product and a live multi-strategy mandate, both of which we believe fit particular niches of investment demand among larger, mainly institutional, investors. We are now planning for these two products to receive a greater proportion of our marketing and sales effort in the coming year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The cost reduction programme initiated in November 2011 resulted in an overall reduction in costs of GBP0.9m and allowed us to control and maintain our operating margin. Whilst the business will remain cost-conscious going forward, further significant cost savings are constrained by the need to maintain an appropriate level of support for investment processes and client services.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Board and senior management\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Following the management reorganisation announced by the business in November 2012, our joint Chief Financial Officer and Chief Operating Officer, Paul Sheriff, left the Company on 15 March 2013 after more than four years on the Board. Paul made an important contribution to the Group over this period, including leading the implementation of a new middle and back office system, and developing the Group's corporate governance and risk framework. On behalf of the Board, I would like to thank Paul for his valuable contribution to the business during this period.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Steve Cullen, previously the Group's Financial Controller for almost 10 years, succeeded Paul on the Board as Chief Financial Officer on 15 March 2013 and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Joel Sleigh\f28 \uc2 \f2  was appointed Chief Operating Officer on the same date. Joel has been with Record for 17 years, and has served as a member of the Executive Committee since 2012. I would like to congratulate Joel on his promotion, and welcome Steve to the Board.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 I would also like to take this opportunity to thank all of the Group's management and staff for their continued hard work and commitment throughout the year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Outlook\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As mentioned above, the Group has been through a prolonged period of change in its product mix, reflecting the outflows of AUME from its long-standing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for return product (based around the Forward Rate Bias) and inflows into the hedging products. During this period, the business has broadened and adapted its suite of products, updated its infrastructure and increased its distribution channels in markets identified as showing good potential for growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As well as standard product offerings, clients are now also interested in more flexible and bespoke solutions. In a competitive environment where the importance of differentiating ourselves and our products is becoming ever more pronounced, we have already started to see the benefits of such investment through increased dialogue with clients and consultants, particularly regarding 'added-value' Hedging products and services, and more flexible multi-strategy solutions and instruments to fulfil individual client's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  requirements. We are well placed to take advantage of such opportunities as they arise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Since the year end, the Group has announced the start of a combined passive and dynamic hedging programme with AUME of approximately $1.7 billion, and its selection for a significant passive hedging mandate (subject to contract), both of which reflect our ability to offer flexible solutions to suit individual client requirements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Neil Record\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chairman\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chief Executive Officer's statement\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Having stabilised the Group's product mix and operating margin, and grown client numbers and AUME over the year, the Group has now established a firm foundation from which to grow.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market overview\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market factors that are most relevant to the Group's performance over the year include continued monetary \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  across developed markets, in particular the United States, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 United Kingdom\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and most recently Japan; the on-going pattern of fluctuating market sentiment and risk appetite; and the tentative re-emergence of trending, at least in some \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs, towards the end of the year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Interest rates remained low across developed market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and central banks continued quantitative easing policies, throughout the year. In particular, the United States Federal Reserve launched a new, open-ended quantitative easing programme in September 2012, and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  launched a series of aggressive measures in the first quarter of 2013.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The three distinct phases of market sentiment most relevant to the Group's business revolve around the perception of crisis across the Eurozone. This ranged from heightened anxiety through until the end of July 2012, when a pledge from European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Mario Draghi "to do whatever it takes to preserve the Euro" established a turning point, and a strong rally in "risk assets". This enthusiasm continued into 2013, until first the inconclusive outcome of the Italian elections and then the Cyprus bail-out reminded the market that the decisions taken in 2012 have yet to be implemented. Risk appetite appears healthy at the start of the new financial year, despite profound concerns regarding the fundamental outlook for developed economies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The latter half of the financial year has also seen the welcome, although tentative, re-emergence of trending in certain \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs - i.e. continued and pronounced movements in one direction over a period of time, in contrast to trading within a relatively narrow range. The Japanese Yen has demonstrated this most clearly, with a marked depreciation since September 2012 first in anticipation, then in response to aggressive policy measures from a new prime minister and Bank of Japan governor.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investment performance\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investment performance across the Group's products reflects their respective characteristics in the light of prevailing market conditions. Dynamic Hedging, which is neither intrinsically "risk on" nor "risk off", has benefited in the latter part of the financial year from the re-emergence of trending, as well as strengthening of base \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the US Dollar and Pound Sterling against exposure \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the Japanese Yen and the Euro.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Both the Emerging Market strategy and the FRB Index strategy, as exemplified by the FTSE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FRB10 Index, performed strongly during the year, in both cases reflecting the divergent interest rate environment between the "major" G5 developed market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and either emerging market or other developed market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  respectively, as well as the bias towards a "risk on" environment over the year as a whole.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Record's first live multi-strategy mandate, which combines Active Forward Rate Bias, emerging markets, value, and momentum, was launched in the second quarter. Whilst the eight months from inception to 31 March 2013 is too short a period from which to draw firm conclusions, investment performance to year-end has been encouraging, with three out of four strategies contributing positively, and some benefits of diversification evident amongst these strategies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asset flows\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 AUME increased by 13% through the year to $34.8 billion. This was due principally to significant new Passive Hedging mandate wins, only marginally offset by client losses, driving net inflows of $1.9 billion. External factors (e.g. equity and other market movements and the impact of exchange rates over the period) also contributed +$2.0 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Strategic progress\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group has continued to develop its product offering, its sales and marketing capability, and its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  infrastructure. [ 11-06-13 0600GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With respect to product development, the Group has continued to invest in enhancing its longer-established products, particularly Hedging, as well as in further developing the new \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Return strategies. Within Passive Hedging in particular, the increasing level of new business interest seen in the second half of the financial year has been accompanied by increased competition, particularly from providers of more basic hedging services at significantly reduced fees. As a premium provider with a long history of bringing intellectual capital to solve clients' problems, the Group's response to this has been to seek to maintain fee rates through product enhancement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With respect to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Return strategies, the launch in the second quarter of the Group's first multi-strategy mandate was an important milestone. Research and development work continues in respect of enhancing individual strategies, as well as the approaches by which they are combined.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group's sales and marketing efforts continue to target those markets which we believe offer the greatest potential for our products - namely institutional investors in Switzerland, the United Kingdom, and in North America. In the United States, we have increased our local presence through the transfer of a member of our research team to Record's Atlanta office. In all geographies, we will continue to ensure that the resources focussed on each market are appropriate for the available opportunities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group also continues to explore new markets which we believe may offer attractive opportunities, whether in adjacent client segments, such as the private wealth management market, or in new client geographies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Our \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  infrastructure also took a significant step forward during the year, with the completion of a long-term project to introduce a new, industry-standard, multi-asset class middle and back office system. This system gives the Group the flexibility to use a much wider range of fixed income and derivative instruments, to continue to offer innovative and value-adding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  management services.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Throughout the financial year we have paid close attention to costs, balancing our investment in the opportunities we see, with maintaining adequate profitability for the Group. This has been reflected in a reduction in the Group's administrative expenses, enabling us to maintain the Group's operating margin at 32%, in line with the prior period.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Outlook\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group's strategic initiatives in recent years have been focussed both on keeping current client mandates and generating sales of new mandates, in order to grow revenues and profits.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The most active area for formal new business procurement processes continues to be Passive Hedging in Switzerland, where we are seeking to capitalise on our long-established client franchise and local sales presence, in order to respond to investors' concerns about hedging implementation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the United States, we continue to focus on helping pension plans and investment consultants understand their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk, which in many cases has not been managed historically, and understand the range of strategies available to manage and exploit \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk and opportunities. In a market with less-established \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  management activity, the pace of progress is necessarily less certain, but the opportunities afforded by the world's largest savings market merit the resources the Group devotes to it.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The United Kingdom continues to be a key market for the Group, albeit one that attracts significant competition for the relatively few mandates available.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Across each of our markets, two forces are making themselves felt. Decision-making processes are typically extended beyond that which has historically prevailed, resulting in a slower rate of new client acquisition. This can likely be attributed to increased due diligence requirements, and a prevailing sense of caution.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Competitive pressures are also being felt across our markets. In some products, such as Passive Hedging, the Group's response has been to seek to maintain fee rates through product enhancement, such as offering a higher level of client service and more bespoke client features. This requires more input from our skilled and committed colleagues, which in turn makes this business less scalable, with lower operating leverage.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In summary, we continue to be confident in the range of opportunities open to the Group, and in the work we have undertaken to best position ourselves to take advantage of these. Our environment will continue to evolve and change, and I am confident that the range of skills and capabilities we have built up will prove adept at responding to these changes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 James Wood-Collins\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chief Executive Officer\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key performance indicators\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Board and Executive Committee use a number of key performance indicators (KPIs) to monitor the performance of the Group. A three-year history of these KPIs is shown below.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  KPIs                             2013         2012         2011 \par -------------------------  -----------  -----------  ----------- \par  AUME at 31 March -            $34.8bn      $30.9bn      $31.4bn \par   US Dollars \par  AUME at 31 March -          GBP22.9bn    GBP19.3bn    GBP19.6bn \par   Sterling \par  Average AUME - US             $32.2bn      $29.2bn      $31.3bn \par   Dollars \par  Client numbers at \par   31 March                          44           41           46 \par  Average management \par   fee rates achieved               8.8         11.2         14.0 \par  Operating profit margin           32%          32%          44% \par  Basic EPS                  1.98 pence   2.23 pence   4.03 pence \par -------------------------  -----------  -----------  ----------- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Summary of highlights\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- AUME increased by $3.9bn (+13%) during the year, standing at $34.8bn at the year end.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- When presented in Sterling, AUME increased by 19% to GBP22.9bn.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Passive and Dynamic Hedging AUME grew by 17% and 11% respectively. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Return AUME reduced by 11%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Average AUME increased by 10% to $32.2bn during the year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Client numbers: this represents the number of separate legal entities that have invested in a Record fund or appointed Record directly as an investment manager. Each entity may have more than one mandate. The number of clients at 31 March 2013 was 44, a net increase of 3 over the year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- The blended average management fee rate fell from 11.2 bps to 8.8 bps due mainly to the change in product mix due to both growth of the Passive Hedging product (which has a lower average management fee rate) and the further decline in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Return.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Record's successful cost reduction programme offset fall in revenue, so the operating profit margin is maintained at last year's level of 32%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- The Group's earnings per share fell to 1.98 pence per share.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Business model\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group was founded in 1983 and this year celebrates 30 years of providing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  management services to institutional investors. The Group has established itself as a respected specialist in this sector both in the eyes of clients and their investment consultants.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group's one operating segment is that of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Management services. The Group employs over 60 employees and its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are performed and controlled centrally from its Head Office based in Windsor, UK.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Products\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group's suite of products is split into two main categories: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Hedging and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Return products.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Hedging comprises Passive Hedging and Dynamic Hedging.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Return includes a range of five \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Return strategies being Active FRB, FRB Index, Emerging Market, Value and Momentum and these strategies can be offered in either a segregated or pooled fund structure. Additionally Record is able to offer combinations of these strategies under a multi-strategy approach that seeks to vary the allocation to these strategies either on a fixed basis or according to market conditions.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Hedging: AUME -- $33.1 billion\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Our hedging products are predominantly systematic in nature. The effectiveness of each of the client mandates is assessed regularly and adjustments are made when necessary in order to respond to changing market conditions. Record has the expertise to deliver tailored hedging programmes to suit the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  needs of our clients. In a competitive market place, this is seen as becoming increasingly important and our work continues in differentiating our hedging products through enhancements to the product offerings.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Passive Hedging: AUME -- $22.1 billion\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 These mandates have the reduction of exposure to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk as their sole objective. They require execution expertise rather than investment judgement or skill. In addition to this execution expertise, clients have been attracted to Record's Passive product offering for our experience and expertise in managing bank counterparty exposure on their behalf, our ability to offer independent trading relationships and our continued innovation with respect to instruments and strategies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dynamic Hedging: AUME -- $11.0 billion\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Record's Dynamic Hedging programme is an attractive alternative to Passive Hedging since these mandates have the reduction of exposure to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk as their principal objective and generating value as a secondary objective. The Dynamic Hedging product seeks to allow our clients to benefit from foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strength while protecting them from foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakness relative to their own base \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Value is generated entirely through the asymmetric reduction of pre-existing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk. Dynamic Hedging's ability to outperform Passive Hedging is dependent on trending in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, rather than any measure of risk appetite.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Return: AUME -- $1.6 billion\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Our \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Return strategies have the generation of investment return as their principal objective. [ 11-06-13 0600GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Record's longer-established return-seeking strategies, the forward rate bias (FRB) and emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategies, are founded on market risk premia and as such perform more strongly in 'risk on' environments. By contrast, value and momentum strategies are founded on market inefficiencies which are more behavioural in nature. As such they are less risk-sensitive, with value strategy returns relatively insensitive to risk sentiment and momentum strategy returns potentially inversely correlated to risk sentiment. It is this diversification of response to risk sentiment that makes the multi-strategy approach an attractive alternative for generating a return from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for potential clients. Furthermore, the multi-strategy approach can be used as a flexible 'overlay' to help clients achieve a variety of investment objectives.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Active FRB -- FY13 performance +1.50%\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The core investment process, the Trend/FRB strategy aims to generate return by buying selected developed market higher interest rate \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and selling selected lower interest rate \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and managing these positions with a view to controlling downside risk.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FRB Index -- FY13 performance +8.20% (fund)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One of Record's medium to long-term aims is to develop \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as an asset class in its own right. The 'asset class project', which started in 2009, saw the launch of a second \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  index, the FTSE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FRB10 Index, in December 2010. Record launched a pooled fund, the Record \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FTSE FRB10 Index Fund, to track this FTSE index and invested GBP1m in December 2010. It is envisaged that a return to more normal monetary policy conditions and a sustained period of positive performance are the precursors to material client demand for this product.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Emerging Market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  --FY13 performance +6.01% (fund)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Record recognises that emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  offer investors an opportunity to either seek to make a return from such \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or seek to separate the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  effect from the underlying overseas domestic asset performance (typically equities or bonds). Record believes that as a result of convergence in the levels of economic output between emerging and developed markets, emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciate over time. This \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation has historically been a significant contributor of returns to (developed market) holders of emerging market assets including equities and bonds. Consequently we regard emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategies as an attractive route through which to implement emerging market local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  debt strategies, offering substantially the same elements of return, with significant implementation benefits including cost, flexibility and security.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This convergence-driven return opportunity combined with the Forward Rate Bias from selection based on interest rate differentials and discretionary risk management, is expected to result in long-term positive performance of the strategy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During the year, the Emerging Market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy passed the three year milestone since 'live' money was invested, with a positive performance of 11.58% since inception. The three year positive track-record is seen as important by investment consultants in gaining acceptance of a new product.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Value --FY13 performance -0.39% (since August 2012)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Research suggests that purchasing power parity (PPP) valuation models have been relatively good predictors of the long-term direction of spot movements. For example, if a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  deviates too much from its equilibrium value (as indicated by PPP), then this deviation will be corrected. The more significant the deviation, the more pressure on the exchange rate to revert to equilibrium value and, consequently, the more rapid the reversion. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  value strategies exploit this insight, buying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that are undervalued relative to PPP and selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that appear overvalued.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Momentum --FY13 performance +2.45% (since August 2012)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This strategy exploits the tendency of the spot rate to appreciate after a prior appreciation, and to depreciate after a previous depreciation. This market inefficiency has persisted across different \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and it is present in other asset classes, such as equities. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is commonly thought of as trending and the momentum strategy seeks to make a return from this phenomenon.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Multi-strategy --FY13 performance +3.54% (since August 2012)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The multi-strategy approach can be applied as an 'overlay' to help clients achieve a variety of investment objectives. Record has the ability to combine four \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  return streams (carry, momentum, value and emerging markets) in different weightings that appeal to particular market segments. Record's first multi-strategy \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  mandate was launched during the year and marks an important strategic step for the Group.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Further information on product investment performance is given in the Operating Review section.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Distribution and clients\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group's sales and marketing activities are organised to ensure that our resources are deployed where opportunities have been identified as giving the most likelihood of future success. To this end, sales and marketing are primarily focussed on the UK, North America and Switzerland with additional activity in continental Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In addition to a full-time sales and marketing team based in the UK headquarters, the Group also has a sales representative based in Switzerland and a sales executive based in the US.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 We distribute through both direct sales to institutional and private wealth clients, and through local and global investment consultants. By working closely with the investment consultants we can promote \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as an asset class and identify new opportunities as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  landscape changes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Our products are delivered both through segregated mandates and also pooled fund structures to suit individual client requirements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Infrastructure\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group's investment function, comprising portfolio implementation, trading and research, is almost entirely based at the Head Office in Windsor, UK.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All of the Group's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and support functions are based at the headquarters in Windsor and include information technology, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , risk management, compliance, finance, legal and human resources.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group has a US sales executive who is based in Atlanta with one member of the research team.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During the year the Group completed its key infrastructure development project, which was the implementation of a new middle and back office system at its headquarters in Windsor. The new system has already increased the Group's capability to both manage a wider variety of instruments and to deploy new products and portfolios cost effectively and will help deliver an improved level of client service in the long term.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Effective risk management and strong internal controls are key aspects of the Group's business model. The Group recognises the inherent risk associated in its business dealings, including the markets and instruments in which it operates. Effective risk management is central to being able to operate effectively and is reflected in the strong internal control culture embedded within the organisation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Business review\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market review\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market factors that are most relevant to the Group's performance over the twelve months to 31 March 2013 include continued monetary \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  across developed markets, in particular the United States, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 United Kingdom\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and most recently Japan; the on-going pattern of alternating periods of risk aversion and risk appetite across markets; and the tentative re-emergence of trending, at least in some \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs, towards the end of the year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Monetary \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 Interest rates remained low across developed market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and central banks continued quantitative easing policies, throughout the year. In particular, the United States Federal Reserve launched a new, open-ended quantitative easing programme in September 2012, under which it committed to purchase an additional $40 billion of mortgage-backed securities each month, as well as signalling its expectation that the federal funds interest rate would be maintained at "exceptionally low levels" at least through to mid 2015. The Bank of England similarly continued its asset purchase programme, authorising purchases of a further GBP50 billion of assets in July 2012, to bring the total purchased to GBP375 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also purchased assets, principally government bonds, from Eurozone banks, albeit with the stated objective of supporting the banks and indirectly the sovereign borrowers, rather than expanding the monetary base. Most recently, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  launched a series of new, aggressive measures in the first quarter of 2013, including explicit targets to double the monetary base, achieve a 2% inflation target within two years, and increased asset purchases and flexibility.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The effect of such measures on broad economic conditions including growth, employment and inflation continues to be uncertain and widely debated. Their impact on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets is more evident - although the suppression of interest rates and hence interest rate differentials between major developed market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has continued, forward rate bias, or "carry" returns were positive. This was because market participants priced in real-term effects of changes in macroeconomic policies (most notably in Japan) and took advantage of the higher rates prevailing amongst other developed market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the Australian and New Zealand Dollars. [ 11-06-13 0600GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 More broadly, such policies are perceived to present a depreciation threat to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , by the very expansion of the monetary base. This depreciation risk has raised fears of "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  wars", by which economies would compete to devalue their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and thereby gain a export-oriented competitive advantage over their trading partners. To date, such monetary expansion has been justified as representing "domestic stimulus" rather than "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  warfare" - it remains to be seen if this can be maintained.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In an environment where such policies are widely pursued amongst major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , allied with other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  actions such as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 's direct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to cap the value of the Swiss Franc, it is impossible for all developed market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  simultaneously to depreciate against each other. Instead exchange rate movements reflect, amongst other things, the market's assessment from time to time of each \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's commitment to further quantitative easing, relative to that of other monetary authorities. As at the start of the new financial year, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  seems to be regarded as the most resolute, with the Federal Reserve potentially the first to suspend or rein in monetary expansion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sentiment and risk appetite\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The three distinct phases of market sentiment most relevant to the Group's business revolve around the perception of crisis across the Eurozone. The financial year began in April 2012 with a heightened sense of crisis, most evidently focussed on the possibility of one or more peripheral countries leaving the Eurozone. In this environment, Neil Record being named as one of five finalists for the Wolfson Economics Prize, which challenged the world's brightest economists to prepare a contingency plan for a break-up of the Eurozone, brought welcome attention to the Group's understanding of the issues.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This "risk off" sentiment broadly persisted until the end of July 2012, with a speech in which European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Mario Draghi pledged "to do whatever it takes to preserve the Euro" being seen as the turning point. Subsequent to this, the market's confidence in the Eurozone authorities' ability and willingness to act, allied with continued liquidity inflows from quantitative easing worldwide, resulted in a strong rally in "risk assets".\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This enthusiasm continued into 2013, until first the inconclusive outcome of the Italian elections in February, followed by the Cyprus bail-out in March, reminded the market that the decisions taken in 2012 have yet to be implemented.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The impact of fluctuating sentiment and risk appetite has been amplified by the continuation throughout much of the year of the "risk on, risk off" phenomenon. In brief, this phenomenon has seen increased correlations amongst assets and instruments, with those offering expected long-term positive returns in exchange for the assumption of risk performing well in "risk on" environments, and those perceived as offering "safe haven" characteristics performing well in "risk off" environments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Amongst the Group's return-seeking strategies, forward rate bias and emerging market strategies characteristically perform more strongly in "risk on" environments, whilst value and momentum strategies are less risk-sensitive, illustrating the benefits of the multi-strategy approach.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Whilst hedging as such is neither "risk on" nor "risk off", this is not necessarily true of investors' base \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . In particular, since the financial crisis, the US Dollar and Japanese Yen have re-asserted their "safe haven" status, typically performing well in "risk off" environments (when equities for example underperform), whilst the Euro and to a lesser extent Pound Sterling have performed better in "risk on" environments. Whilst this heightened correlation - whether positive or negative - between equity and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements persists, it will subtly alter arguments around \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hedging, such that the rationale for a US Dollar investor to hedge the (correlated) \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk overlaid on international equities has strengthened, whilst that for a Euro investor has weakened.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As at the start of the new financial year, risk appetite appears healthy, despite profound concerns regarding the fundamental outlook for developed economies. There has also been debate as to whether the "risk on, risk off" phenomenon itself has run its course. Divergent economic performance among the major developed economies has resulted in correlations weakening, but in the Group's view it is too soon to call an end to this phenomenon.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Trending\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The latter half of the financial year has also seen the welcome, although tentative, re-emergence of trending in certain \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs - i.e. continued and pronounced movements in one direction over a period of time. This behaviour seemed to have been suppressed, or at least altered, in the range-trading environment that prevailed through much of 2011 and the first half of 2012.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese Yen has demonstrated the re-emergence of trending most clearly. Since the September 2012 election of Shinzo Abe as the Prime Minister of Japan, and accelerating with the first meeting of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's monetary policy committee under new Governor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Haruhiko Kuroda\f28 \uc2 \f2  shortly after the end of the Group's financial year, the Japanese Yen has been on a firm downwards trend, weakening more than 20% against the US Dollar. Since the Group's (non-Japanese) hedging clients will be short Yen in their hedging programmes, and each of the forward rate bias, emerging markets and momentum strategies are short Yen, on its own this has been a rewarding move for the Group's clients.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 More broadly the re-emergence, at least on a qualified basis, of trending is welcome as trending is the source of Dynamic Hedging's long-term outperformance over Passive Hedging. Trending is also the pattern of behaviour exploited in Record's momentum strategies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Operating review\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Product investment performance\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Our hedging products are predominantly systematic in nature. The effectiveness of each of the client mandates is assessed regularly and adjustments are made when necessary in order to respond to changing market conditions or to bring the risk profile of the hedging mandate in line with the client's risk tolerance.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The performance of our Dynamic Hedging product depends on how the foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  change in value relative to the base \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of a client. During the last year, mandates for our US and UK-based Dynamic Hedging clients performed as expected in terms of allowing clients to benefit from periods of strengthening foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , whilst being protected against periods of weakening foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 UK investors saw gains from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  over the year as Sterling weakened, leading to higher base valuations for foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  investments. UK-based Dynamic Hedging programmes progressively lowered hedge ratios over the year in response to Sterling weakness; this resulted in hedging delivering only a modest negative return, allowing the majority of the gains from foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exposure to be preserved.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Conversely, US investors saw losses from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on international exposures when valuing positions in US Dollars, as foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakened. Record's Dynamic Hedging product generated high hedge ratios to capture US Dollar strength, leading to positive returns from hedging, in turn helping to offset losses from international \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exposure.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Record had five 'live' \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Return strategies in the year. The Active Forward Rate Bias (FRB), and Emerging Market strategies are both active and return-seeking in nature, although they exploit different market phenomena. The third, fourth and fifth return-seeking strategies are more passive in nature: the Record FTSE FRB10 Index Fund, a strategy that tracks the FTSE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FRB10 index, and the value and momentum strategies. Any combination of these \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Return strategies may be combined within a multi-strategy mandate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the Active FRB strategy, the core investment process - the Trend/Forward Rate Bias strategy - aims to buy selected developed market higher interest rate \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and sell selected lower interest rate \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and to manage these positions with a view to controlling downside risk. Historically this investment approach has shown positive returns due to the existence of the Forward Rate Bias and trending movements in selected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs. The year saw positive returns as relatively low interest rate \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , particularly the Japanese Yen, weakened. A complementary investment process - the Range Trading strategy - generated a negative return as selected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs failed to mean revert, partially offsetting the positive performance of the Trend/Forward Rate Bias strategy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Similarly, the Forward Rate Bias Index strategy added value as high interest rate \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciated. Record remains committed to the belief that over time \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and in particular the FRB can be a persistent and uncorrelated source of returns for investors, and that the FRB will continue to generate long-term returns.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Record's Emerging Market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Fund generated positive returns over the period as emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciated against a basket of developed market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . A perceived reduction in the risk of outright crises in the Eurozone area and signs of growth emerging from the US combined to provide a platform for emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation. This was compounded by actions in Japan to weaken the Yen, further boosting programme returns.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Record's only live multi-strategy mandate outperformed over the period. Gains were led by returns in the emerging market strand, with positive contributions from the FRB and momentum strategies. Modest negative returns from the value strand had a small offsetting impact on performance. [ 11-06-13 0600GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Record's Euro Stress Fund was terminated during the year. The decision to close the Fund came about following the ECB's announcement of a "fully effective backstop" through unlimited bond purchases, supportive verdicts from the German Constitutional Court and the Dutch electorate, and the resumption of QE 3 by the Federal Reserve which brought about a rally in risk assets and the Euro itself. While we continue to share widespread doubts about the ultimate viability of the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , coordinated actions within the Eurozone have arrested immediate concerns of further decline, and lessened the opportunity to capitalise on such concerns through \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Annual returns of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Return products; year to 31 March 2013\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  Fund Name                   Gearing   Annual Return         Volatility \par                                                    %    since inception \par                                                               % p.a.(1) \par --------------------------  --------  --------------  ----------------- \par  FTSE FRB 10 Index Fund \par   (2)                            1.8           8.20%              7.50% \par  Emerging Market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par   (3)                              1           6.01%              8.04% \par  Index /composite returns              Annual Return         Volatility \par                                                    %    since inception \par                                                                  % p.a. \par --------------------------  --------  --------------  ----------------- \par  Alpha composite (4)                           1.50%              2.70% \par  FTSE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FRB10 GBP \par   Excess return                                4.38%              4.70% \par  Record multi-strategy                         3.54%                N/A \par   (since Aug-2012) \par --------------------------  --------  --------------  ----------------- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (1)No volatility data is provided for products with less than 12 months historic data\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (2)FTSE FRB10 Index Fund return data is since inception in December 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (3)Emerging Market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Fund return data is since inception in December 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (4) An investment return track record generated by the aggregation of all standard segregated track records for Record's active forward rate bias \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Return product. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Alpha composite is asset weighted, based on AUME for each account\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gearing\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Return product group allows clients to pick the level of exposure they desire in their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  programmes. The pooled funds have historically offered clients a range of gearing and target volatility levels. The segregated mandates allow clients to individually pick the level of gearing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It should be emphasised that in this case 'gearing' refers to the multiple of the maximum size of the aggregate forward contracts in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  programme, to the pooled fund's net assets or the segregated mandate size. This is limited by the willingness of counterparty banks to take exposure to the pooled fund or segregated client. Gearing in this context does not involve borrowing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 AUME development\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group has seen an aggregate increase in AUME of $3.9bn (+13%) to finish the year at AUME of $34.8bn compared with $30.9bn at the end of the previous year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 AUME development ($bn)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  Opening AuME   Net client   Markets   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  effect   Closing AuME \par   at 1 April       flows                            at 31 March \par      2012                                              2013 \par -------------  -----------  --------  ----------  ------------- \par      30.9          +1.9       +3.2       -1.2          34.8 \par -------------  -----------  --------  ----------  ------------- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 AUME movements result both from factors within Record's control and external factors outside its control. External factors include exchange rates and the underlying asset value (usually equities) on which many segregated mandates are based. External factors accounted for a net increase of $2.0bn in AUME during the year, made up of:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (i) an increase of $3.2bn related to movements in global stock and other markets as many mandate sizes are linked to such markets;\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (ii) a fall of $1.2bn due to changes in exchange rates over the period, which affects the conversion of non-US Dollar mandate sizes into US Dollar AUME. This movement does not have an equivalent impact on the Sterling value of fee income.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group has seen net inflows of $1.9bn from clients. Inflows from both new and existing clients totalled $5.1bn, and were offset by outflows of $3.2bn.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 When expressed in Sterling, AUME increased by GBP3.6bn (+19%) to finish the year at GBP22.9bn (2012: GBP19.3bn). The split of AUME at the year end by base \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was 25% in Sterling, 49% in Swiss Francs, 20% in US Dollars, 5% in Euros and 1% in Canadian Dollars.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dynamic Hedging AUME increased by 11% to $11.0bn (2012: $9.9bn), which was mainly the result of growth in value of the underlying assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Since the year end, the Group has announced the start of a new combined passive and dynamic hedging programme for a UK-based client with AUME totalling approximately $1.7 billion, including a performance fee element.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As in 2012, Passive Hedging AUME increased by $3.2bn, a 17% increase in the year (2012: 20%). This increase was principally due to the combination of two factors: net inflows of $2.0bn, and a growth of $2.1bn in value of the underlying assets, typically international equities and bonds, offset by movements in exchange rates of $0.9bn.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Record's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Return AUME fell to $1.6bn (2012: $1.8bn) due principally to the net outflows which totalled $0.3bn. Included in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Return mandates are a small number of mandates for emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  totalling $0.8bn (2012: $0.8bn) and a single multi-strategy mandate of $0.3bn, which started in the year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Product mix\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group's product mix has shown a period of stability following a prolonged period of change. Over the last four years, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Return AUME as a proportion of total AUME has decreased from 43% to 5%, as a result of the challenging environment for the Active FRB product, and consequent outflows. Conversely both Passive and Dynamic Hedging have seen increases in AUME over the same period of 70% and 175% respectively.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During the year, Hedging grew to 95% of AUME (2012: 93%), as a result of the growth of the Passive Hedging product, which accounted for 63% of AUME (2012: 61%), and the stability of the Dynamic Hedging product which accounted for 32% of AUME (2012: 32%). Together \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Return and Dynamic Hedging represented 37% of AUME (2012: 38%) being higher margin products compared to Passive Hedging. \f28 \b \hig